unsupervised analysis methods can help  us understand  structure distance and similarity in our  data  here i have a data set of gene  expression that has  a number of genes six 6918  and a number of samples 52.  these samples can be organized into  different groups  and inside each gene i have its gene  expression values  in fbkm values now if i would like to  understand the relationship between  two genes i can simply make a scatter  plot  and then i will know the relationship  between the expression values of  one vector of a gene and another vector  of another gene  i can do the same for two particular  samples  just looking at two samples i can create  another scatter plot  and see the relationship between them i  would expect  similar genes to have similar expression  and similar samples  that fall under the same group to also  have  a similar expression pattern but when i  come to a data set that has  thousands of genes across tens or  hundreds of samples it's really  difficult to get a grasp or a snapshot  of what that really means unsupervised  analysis of big data can help us  identify patterns relationships and  important structures inside of our data  set  to do that we can go to the unsupervised  analysis section  and upload the data that i was just  showing  since our data is annotated by group i'm  going to press on continue  and select start  pcar library here i have a number of  parameters that i don't really have to  change but what they mean  is the number of output principal  components that i will get in my chart  whether i want to scale or not the data  that i am putting in  do i want to center the data and whether  the data has to be transposed in other  words do i want to look at the  relationship between  samples or objects in my data or genes  in other words  features or rows in my data and so i'm  going to say yes to analyze the  relationship between  samples which are annotated in my data  set  now i will click on end after i click on  end i'm going to give it a second to  create the pipeline  i will name it  save it and now i can run this pipeline  on the cluster  it might take a little bit for the data  set to process depending on the size of  the data set  and the number of users running this  analysis at the same time on the shared  cluster  as soon as the pipeline has completed i  can click on download pipeline output  files  and find some results that include text  files  pdf files and also a packaged  r script that i can use let's take a  look at the plots in pdf  remember their principal component  analysis is a dimensionality reduction  technique  so what we see is that whole data set  reduced to just  two dimensions principle component one  and principal component two  inside these two dimensions we will find  dots that represent  objects or samples in the data set the  distances that we observe  represent a similarity between samples  so when they are closer together  they are more alike and when they are  far apart they are more  different from each other this  difference is based on variance  in the sample data itself so whenever we  see  some two dots closer together that means  that  they are not as varied  compared to the rest of the data set  and that's what you see here in these  percentages  next to pc1 and pc2 this is the total  variance represented by each component  so the variance of pc1 and pc2 together  or the full plot is 12.62  plus 18.08 or about 30 percent  now in the next two pdf plots you will  find  that the same data could be represented  on pc1  and pc3 together representing about  20 25 of the data and  pc3 and pc2 some of these combinations  of principal components show  a much better separation between samples  based on group  based on the annotation that we provided  to the analysis method  so here you can see bazel cloud and low  luminal and normal-like  cloud and low are fairly separate from  all the rest  as well as all of the other colors are  clearly separated  within this principle component space  analyzing the data in such a way can  lead us to generate a novel hypothesis  the hypothesis might be that all the  groups are different  and the biggest difference is between  the luminal  and all of the other samples combined  given this insight we can now test our  hypothesis  developing a supervised analysis method  that would be looking at the differences  between these two groups
 

